 routing cases in `test/hil/test_routing_dense.py`. Companion to
 `ROUTING_SWEEP_2026-09-03.md` (the firmware side of the same night).
 
+**Since 2026-11-22 these proposals can be tried.** The routing lab
+(`tools/routing_lab/README.md`, "Arrangements" and "The lane ranking") takes
+a crossbar arrangement as a small file of what differs from V5, draws it,
+and scores it with an exact solver against V5 on the firmware's own
+netlists. P1 + P2 of this document is the file
+`tools/routing_lab/arrangements/v6_rails_on_chips.json`. Measured that day
+(the exact solver, each bridge on its own path of at most four crosspoints,
+probe feed off the fabric, the 44 netlists of the dense, real and stress
+classes): all 44 are routable on both; over the 38 whose fewest crosspoints
+is proved on both, P1 + P2 needs 861 and V5's copper 955. Of two random
+netlists grown until the solver could prove no more, one reaches 54 bridges
+on both and the other 65 on P1 + P2 against 62. That is what the copper can
+carry, not what a V6 router will route. The lab also ranks V5's 94 lanes,
+bounce rows and hub lanes by what the present router loses without each and
+by whether any netlist is proved to need it. With the feed off, over 244
+netlists (those 44 and 200 everyday-sized ones), the only wires any netlist
+needs, taking one wire away at a time, are the eight lanes into K, and the
+one netlist that needs them is `k_rows_overflow_metric`. The present router
+loses most without the lanes to the SF chips and next to nothing without any
+one lane between two breadboard chips. The README's findings 11 to 16 have
+the numbers.
+
 Vocabulary used throughout:
 
 * **row** - a breadboard row on a breadboard chip's Y pin (chips A-H, Y1-Y7).
